tmux integration: never send server-controlled values as commands (CVE-2019-9535).

Backported from upstream b316c29f4b (3.3.6), "Do not send
server-controlled values in tmux integration mode", adapted to 3.0.15.

- Sessions are targeted by number ($N) everywhere: attach, kill, rename,
  list-windows, link-window, new-window and rename-window. A session's
  name is the server's to choose, and it used to be spliced into these
  commands; four of them did not even escape it. list-sessions now
  reports "#{session_id} #{session_name}", and the dashboard's tables
  carry iTermTmuxSessionObject (number and name) and show the name only.
  Upstream added iTermTmuxSessionObject as its own file; here it lives in
  TmuxController.[hm], so the Xcode project's source list stands.
- Options this app stores in the server (@affinities, @origins, @hidden)
  are written hex-encoded behind upstream's prefixes (a_, o_, i_). A
  value returned by the server can therefore never close the quotes of
  the command that writes it back. Plain values from upstream iTerm2 or
  an older build are still read; a malformed encoded value reads as
  empty. The encoding is of UTF-8 bytes: NSString's hexEncodedString
  encodes UTF-16 units, which do not survive a round trip for non-ASCII.
- A window name typed by the user is escaped as one quoted argument.
- 3.0.15 has none of what upstream's fix also changed for set-titles-
  string, status-left/right polling, hotkeys or tab colors.

